  "final_text": "I created the dashboard and the alert rule, and both are in Kibana. The rule has already run once and reported status `ok`, so its query is accepted, but I haven't seen it fire. I also haven't opened the dashboard to check that each panel shows data.\n\n**1. Fields.** The `Kibana Sample Data Logs` data view uses `timestamp` as its time field. Each document is one web request. It records:\n- the response code (`response`, a string)\n- the size of the response (`bytes`)\n- the visitor's address (`clientip`)\n- the URL requested (`url`, `request`)\n- file type (`extension`)\n- where the visitor came from (`geo.src`, `geo.dest`) and the referrer (`referer`)\n- operating system (`machine.os`) and browser (`agent`)\n- tags and hour of day\n\n**2. Dashboard: \"EFF-6ebbbe4f Web logs overview\".** It has 7 panels and shows the last 7 days by default. Link: http://localhost:15601/app/dashboards#/view/eff-6ebbbe4f-web-logs-overview-cdff0fe03cc7\n- Single numbers for total requests, unique visitors (distinct client IPs) and total bytes served\n- Requests over time, split by response code (stacked bars)\n- Share of each response code (pie chart)\n- Top 10 countries visitors come from (bar chart)\n- Top 10 requested URLs, with request count and bytes (table)\n\n**3. Alert rule: \"EFF-6ebbbe4f 5xx spike\".** The rule ID is `7717f4f7-7e93-4804-a097-895496fac5ad`.\n- It searches `kibana_sample_data_logs` every minute using `timestamp`.\n- It fires when more than 10 requests with a 5xx code arrive in the last 5 minutes.\n- Because the response code is stored as text, it finds 5xx codes by matching the values from \"500\" up to (but not including) \"600\". That works because the codes are always three digits.\n- I turned it on when I created it (new rules start off by default).\n- It has no actions attached, so when it fires it raises an alert in Kibana but sends no notification. If you want an email or Slack message, I'd need to create a connector and attach it.",
